  public int sharedDrawersPositiveInkInvoice(int[][] drawers, int k) {
    int[][] a=drawers; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Set<Integer>right=new HashSet<>();for(int x:a[1])right.add(x);Set<Integer>both=new TreeSet<>();for(int x:a[0])if(right.contains(x))both.add(x);out.addAll(both);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>0)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

drawers contains two lists of numbers. Find the numbers that appear in both lists. Put each matching number in the result list only once. Sort the result list from smallest to largest.

Return the answer

Add only numbers greater than 0 from the result list. Do not add 0 or negative numbers. Return the total, or 0 if no numbers match.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n + d log d); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the combined length of the two lists; d is the number of different values. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

